      "anchor": "t32",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "A first-word index for big rule groups",
      "excerpt": "A first-word index for big rule groups",
      "text": "Do it.  In fact, if there are ANY (small set of) constant words in the sequence you should be able to do an immediate elimination of the rule by just scanning the line.  What would this do to speed?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-04T02:00:21.071Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-04T02:01:45.886Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, doing it.  Your version is better than the first-word index:  each line's words go into a set ONCE, and a rule whose syntax has words it can't do without (<code>set ... to</code>, <code>is a menu of</code>, <code>starting at</code>) is skipped at once when any of them is missing from the line.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Safe:  only words outside optional parts count, so it can only skip a rule that could never match.</li>\n  <li>The catch:  most rules write their own <code>parse()</code>, so each one says which words it needs (or the check reads them off its syntax);  a rule that says nothing is still tried.</li>\n  <li>What it does to speed:  not known until measured.  P21 counted 2.3 million rule tries on the tests, most turned away by their quick test;  this turns most of those away for the cost of a set lookup.  The build reports it with <code>speedtest --by-module</code>, before and after.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Queued after P24, before P26:  the build agent is on P23 and P24 now.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-04T02:01:23.398Z"
      }
    }
  },
